body:
## Summary

Adds a new MCP tool — `update_partial_workflow` — that updates a workflow by applying a small list of named operations directly to the stored JSON, bypassing the full SDK code → JSON parse + LLM-validate pass that `update_workflow` runs on every edit.

The existing `update_workflow` tool requires the agent to re-emit the full SDK code on every edit, which scales linearly with workflow size. Iterating on a 30-node workflow to tweak one parameter still costs ~5–10 KB of output tokens per call. The partial-update path keeps per-edit payloads small and constant in size — the same parameter tweak ships ~150 chars regardless of workflow size.

Linear: https://linear.app/n8n/issue/ADO-5171

### Operations (v1)

The tool takes an `operations: []` array. Each op is one of:

```
updateNodeParameters   — change parameters on a node (deep-merge or replace)
addNode                — insert a new node
removeNode             — delete a node + all its connections
renameNode             — rename + rewrite connection references
addConnection          — wire two nodes (defaults: type=main, indices=0,0)
removeConnection       — unwire two nodes
setNodeCredential      — attach a credential to a node
setNodePosition        — move a node on the canvas
setNodeDisabled        — toggle a node
setWorkflowMetadata    — update workflow name and/or description
```

The whole batch is **atomic**: if any op fails (missing node, duplicate name, missing connection) the workflow is left unchanged and a `Operation N failed: <reason>` error is returned with the offending op index.

### Why a new tool, not a replacement

The two tools have different signatures and serve different jobs. `update_workflow` (full rewrite) stays for major restructuring; `update_partial_workflow` is preferred for parameter tweaks, single-node insertions, rewiring, rename, and metadata edits. n8n-mcp ships both; their telemetry shows 89.2% of update calls go through the partial path, 10.8% through the full one. The MCP instructions guide agents to prefer partial and fall back to full when the change is too large to express compactly.

### Token economy (measured on the live test in this PR)

3 typical iterative edits, replayed via both tools on the same workflow:

| Edit | `update_workflow` | `update_partial_workflow` | Reduction |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Set parameters on one node | 610 chars / ~153 tok | 163 chars / ~41 tok | **3.7× smaller** (73.3%) |
| Add a node + connect it | 797 chars / ~200 tok | 232 chars / ~58 tok | **3.4× smaller** (70.9%) |
| Rename a node + set description | 1,129 chars / ~283 tok | 313 chars / ~79 tok | **3.6× smaller** (72.3%) |
| **Total** | **2,536 chars** | **708 chars** | **3.6× smaller (72.1%)** |

This is on a tiny 2–3 node workflow — the smallest possible sample. The gap widens with workflow size: per the n8n-mcp competitive analysis (May 2026), the same edits at 15 nodes show ~15× reduction and at 30 nodes ~22×, with a 41× median across 30K real production mutations.

#### How to replicate the numbers

The benchmark is pure JSON math — no server or auth needed. Save the script below to a file and run with Node ≥ 18 (uses `structuredClone`-equivalent JSON only, no special deps):

```bash
node ./ado-5171-token-bench.mjs
```

Expected output:

```
ADO-5171 — token reduction per edit (live test replication)

  Test 1 — set parameters on one node                    610 chars /     153 tok       163 chars /      41 tok   3.7× smaller (73.3% reduction)
  Test 2 — add a node + connect it                       797 chars /     200 tok       232 chars /      58 tok   3.4× smaller (70.9% reduction)
  Test 3 — rename a node + set description              1129 chars /     283 tok       313 chars /      79 tok   3.6× smaller (72.3% reduction)

Across the 3 edits combined:
  update_workflow         total: 2536 chars / ~634 tok (estimate)
  update_partial_workflow total: 708 chars / ~177 tok (estimate)
  Reduction: 3.6× smaller, 72.1% fewer chars
```

The script defines the same 2-node demo workflow we tested live, evolves it through the 3 edits two ways (full SDK code rewrite vs. partial ops), and prints char/token counts for each. Anyone reviewing the PR can run it and get identical numbers — no setup required.

<details>
<summary>📋 <code>ado-5171-token-bench.mjs</code></summary>

```javascript
// Replicable token-reduction benchmark for ADO-5171.
// Re-runs the 3 edits from the live MCP test, computing payload sizes for both
// the existing `update_workflow` (full SDK rewrite) and the new
// `update_partial_workflow` (small ops). Pure JSON math — no server required.

// ---------- Workflow state, evolved through the same edits as the live test ----------

const wf0 = `import { workflow, node, trigger } from '@n8n/workflow-sdk';

const startTrigger = trigger({
  type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger',
  version: 1,
  config: { name: 'Manual Trigger', position: [240, 300] },
  output: [{}]
});

const editFields = node({
  type: 'n8n-nodes-base.set',
  version: 3.4,
  config: { name: 'Edit Fields', parameters: {}, position: [540, 300] },
  output: [{}]
});

export default workflow('ado-5171-demo', 'ADO-5171 Partial Update Demo')
  .add(startTrigger)
  .to(editFields);
`;

// After Test 1 (set parameters on Edit Fields):
const wf1 = wf0.replace(
  "parameters: {}, position: [540, 300]",
  "parameters: { mode: 'manual', includeOtherFields: false }, position: [540, 300]",
);

// After Test 2 (add NoOp + connect):
const wf2 = `import { workflow, node, trigger } from '@n8n/workflow-sdk';

const startTrigger = trigger({
  type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger',
  version: 1,
  config: { name: 'Manual Trigger', position: [240, 300] },
  output: [{}]
});

const editFields = node({
  type: 'n8n-nodes-base.set',
  version: 3.4,
  config: { name: 'Edit Fields', parameters: { mode: 'manual', includeOtherFields: false }, position: [540, 300] },
  output: [{}]
});

const afterEdit = node({
  type: 'n8n-nodes-base.noOp',
  version: 1,
  config: { name: 'After Edit', parameters: {}, position: [840, 300] },
  output: [{}]
});

export default workflow('ado-5171-demo', 'ADO-5171 Partial Update Demo')
  .add(startTrigger)
  .to(editFields)
  .to(afterEdit);
`;

// After Test 3 (rename Edit Fields -> Process, set description):
const wf3 = wf2
  .replace(/Edit Fields/g, "Process")
  .replace(/editFields/g, "process_")
  .replace(
    "'ADO-5171 Partial Update Demo')",
    "'ADO-5171 Partial Update Demo', { description: 'Demo workflow created via update_partial_workflow tool — exercises updateNodeParameters, addNode, addConnection, renameNode, and atomic batch behavior.' })",
  );

// ---------- Payloads sent for each tool ----------

const fullWorkflowCall = (workflowId, code, extras = {}) =>
  JSON.stringify({ workflowId, code, ...extras });

const partialWorkflowCall = (workflowId, operations) =>
  JSON.stringify({ workflowId, operations });

const WF_ID = "5qNG3TO1vOdamBlH";

const tests = [
  {
    label: "Test 1 — set parameters on one node",
    full: fullWorkflowCall(WF_ID, wf1),
    partial: partialWorkflowCall(WF_ID, [
      {
        type: "updateNodeParameters",
        nodeName: "Edit Fields",
        parameters: { mode: "manual", includeOtherFields: false },
      },
    ]),
  },
  {
    label: "Test 2 — add a node + connect it",
    full: fullWorkflowCall(WF_ID, wf2),
    partial: partialWorkflowCall(WF_ID, [
      {
        type: "addNode",
        node: {
          name: "After Edit",
          type: "n8n-nodes-base.noOp",
          typeVersion: 1,
          position: [840, 300],
        },
      },
      { type: "addConnection", source: "Edit Fields", target: "After Edit" },
    ]),
  },
  {
    label: "Test 3 — rename a node + set description",
    full: fullWorkflowCall(WF_ID, wf3, {
      description:
        "Demo workflow created via update_partial_workflow tool — exercises updateNodeParameters, addNode, addConnection, renameNode, and atomic batch behavior.",
    }),
    partial: partialWorkflowCall(WF_ID, [
      { type: "renameNode", oldName: "Edit Fields", newName: "Process" },
      {
        type: "setWorkflowMetadata",
        description:
          "Demo workflow created via update_partial_workflow tool — exercises updateNodeParameters, addNode, addConnection, renameNode, and atomic batch behavior.",
      },
    ]),
  },
];

// ---------- Report ----------

const tok = (s) => Math.ceil(s.length / 4); // standard char/4 estimate
const fmt = (n) => n.toString().padStart(7);
const pct = (a, b) => `${(((a - b) / a) * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const ratio = (a, b) => `${(a / b).toFixed(1)}×`;

console.log("\nADO-5171 — token reduction per edit (live test replication)\n");

let totalFullChars = 0;
let totalPartialChars = 0;

for (const { label, full, partial } of tests) {
  const fc = full.length;
  const pc = partial.length;
  totalFullChars += fc;
  totalPartialChars += pc;
  console.log(
    `  ${label.padEnd(50)} ${fmt(fc)} chars / ${fmt(tok(full))} tok       ${fmt(pc)} chars / ${fmt(tok(partial))} tok       ${ratio(fc, pc)} smaller (${pct(fc, pc)} reduction)`,
  );
}

console.log(`\nAcross the 3 edits combined:`);
console.log(`  update_workflow         total: ${totalFullChars} chars / ~${tok({ length: totalFullChars })} tok (estimate)`);
console.log(`  update_partial_workflow total: ${totalPartialChars} chars / ~${tok({ length: totalPartialChars })} tok (estimate)`);
console.log(`  Reduction: ${ratio(totalFullChars, totalPartialChars)} smaller, ${pct(totalFullChars, totalPartialChars)} fewer chars\n`);

console.log("Notes:");
console.log("  • Token estimate uses chars / 4 (standard rough heuristic).");
console.log("  • Sizes are JSON request bodies — what the agent emits as output tokens.");
console.log("  • update_workflow re-sends the full SDK code on every edit, so its size");
console.log("    grows linearly with workflow size; partial-update size stays roughly flat.");
console.log("  • For real-world workflows (15-30 nodes with rich parameters), the gap grows:");
console.log("    n8n-mcp's competitive analysis measured 6.5×-22× at 4-30 nodes,");
console.log("    and a 41× median across 30K real production mutations.");
```

</details>

### Scope

The new tool is registered alongside `update_workflow` inside `registerBuilderTools()`, behind the same `N8N_MCP_BUILDER_ENABLED` flag — instances that don't enable the builder MCP surface are unchanged.

### Implementation notes

- `workflow-operations.ts` is the pure ops engine — discriminated zod union, atomic `applyOperations(workflow, ops)`. Easy to unit-test, no I/O.
- The tool handler reuses existing infrastructure: `getMcpWorkflow` for permission/MCP-availability, `collaborationService.ensureWorkflowEditable` for write-lock, `resolveNodeWebhookIds` for added webhook nodes, `workflowService.update` for save, `collaborationService.broadcastWorkflowUpdate` for cross-editor notification.
- Credential auto-assignment runs **only on nodes added in the same batch** (not on untouched existing nodes), so partial updates never silently mutate credential state on nodes the agent didn't touch.

## Test plan

- [x] Unit tests: 28 op-level (`workflow-operations.test.ts`) + 14 handler-level (`update-partial-workflow.tool.test.ts`) — all passing
- [x] Sibling tests still passing (`update-workflow.tool.test.ts`, `mcp.service.test.ts`, `credentials-auto-assign.test.ts`)
- [x] `pnpm typecheck` clean on `packages/cli` and `packages/@n8n/ai-workflow-builder.ee`
- [x] `pnpm lint` clean on changed files
- [x] **End-to-end live test** against a local n8n instance (`N8N_MCP_BUILDER_ENABLED=true`): created a workflow via `create_workflow_from_code`, then exercised `updateNodeParameters`, `addNode`+`addConnection`, `renameNode`+`setWorkflowMetadata`, plus a deliberate atomic-failure case. State verified via `get_workflow_details`. Atomic rollback confirmed (post-failure `versionId` and node positions unchanged).
- [x] I have seen this code, I have run this code, and I take responsibility for this code.

## Summary

**Problem**: Workflows created via the API could be malformed (e.g., missing node fields, broken connections, or duplicate names) yet still be saved successfully. When opened in the editor, these workflows would crash with the error "Could not find workflow." This failure was effectively invisible to users - the error message disappeared almost immediately, and they were redirected to a new workflow page without any clear indication of what went wrong.

**Solution**: This PR adds a shared structural validator in `n8n-workflow` that is the single source of truth for what constitutes a valid workflow shape. It is intentionally limited to structure only (required fields, correct types, graph integrity) and does not touch activation/semantic validation.

**Backend**:
- rejects malformed payloads on create, update, and import with a `400` and detailed issue description.

**Frontend**:
- **Open existing workflow / import (file/paste/URL)**: non-blocking. Sanitizes malformed data (filters nodes without `type`, fixes bad positions, strips broken connections) and renders the remaining valid nodes. Shows a warning toast. If `initializeWorkspace` still throws, the error is reported to Sentry via `console.error` as a safety net.
- **Template from JSON**: blocking. Rejects invalid data and shows an error toast without rendering.

Additionally, both the open and import paths now sanitize malformed data to prevent canvas rendering crashes:
- Nodes with missing `type` are filtered out (with a toast showing the count of skipped nodes on import).
- Nodes with missing or malformed `position` are defaulted to `[0, 0]` so they still render.
- Malformed connections (non-array buckets, non-array inner entries) are stripped to prevent `TypeError` crashes in `mapConnectionsByDestination` and canvas edge rendering.

## How to test

Paste the helper into the browser console while logged in, then run each test case. All should return `400` with a descriptive error.
To test frontend behavior, create the same workflows on master (requests would pass) and try to access them from the workflow list. All of them should be accessible and for most of them an error toast should be shown.

<details>
<summary>Helper</summary>

```js
const createWorkflow = async (body) => {
  const res = await fetch('/rest/workflows', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'browser-id': localStorage.getItem('n8n-browserId'),
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  console.log(res.status, json);
  return json;
};
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>1. Node missing <code>type</code></summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Missing type',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>2. Node missing <code>name</code></summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Missing name',
  nodes: [
    { type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>3. Node missing <code>position</code></summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Missing position',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>4 Position is not a 2-number tuple</summary>

```js
// Single number
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Bad position - 1 number',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});

// Three numbers (worked before fine as well)
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Bad position - 3 numbers',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300, 400], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});

// Strings instead of numbers
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Bad position - strings',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: ['a', 'b'], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>5. Duplicate node names</summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Duplicate node names',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.noOp', position: [450, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {},
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>6 Malformed nested connections</summary>

```js
// Inner value is a string instead of the expected array-of-arrays
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Malformed connections - string value',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {
    Start: { main: 'not-an-array' },
  },
});

// Inner bucket contains a raw object instead of array of connection entries
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Malformed connections - object in bucket',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {
    Start: { main: [{ node: 'Foo', type: 'main', index: 0 }] },
  },
});

// Connection entry missing required fields
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Malformed connections - incomplete entry',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
    { name: 'NoOp', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.noOp', position: [450, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {
    Start: { main: [[{ node: 'NoOp' }]] },
  },
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>7. Connection source does not match any node</summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Unknown connection source',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
    { name: 'NoOp', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.noOp', position: [450, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {
    NonExistentNode: { main: [[{ node: 'NoOp', type: 'main', index: 0 }]] },
  },
});
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>8. Connection target does not match any node</summary>

```js
await createWorkflow({
  name: 'Unknown connection target',
  nodes: [
    { name: 'Start', type: 'n8n-nodes-base.manualTrigger', position: [250, 300], parameters: {} },
  ],
  settings: {},
  connections: {
    Start: { main: [[{ node: 'NonExistentTarget', type: 'main', index: 0 }]] },
  },
});
```
</details>


