## Summary
Refactor `addUsers` function in users store to use `reduce` instead of `forEach`, triggering only a single reactive update instead of N updates when adding N users.

**Performance optimization:**
- **Reduces reactive update complexity from O(N) to O(1)**
- Minimizes Vue reactivity overhead (dependency tracking and update dispatching)
- Significantly improves performance when loading user lists or bulk inviting users

## Code Changes

### Before
```typescript
const addUsers = (newUsers: User[]) => {
  newUsers.forEach((userResponse) => {
    const prevUser = usersById.value[userResponse.id] || {};
    const updatedUser = { ...prevUser, ...userResponse };
    const user: IUser = {
      ...updatedUser,
      fullName: userResponse.firstName
        ? `${updatedUser.firstName} ${updatedUser.lastName || ''}`
        : undefined,
      isDefaultUser: _isDefaultUser(updatedUser),
      isPendingUser: _isPendingUser(updatedUser),
    };

    // ❌ Triggers reactive update on EACH iteration
    usersById.value = {
      ...usersById.value,
      [user.id]: user,
    };
  });
};
```

**Performance issue:** When adding N users, this triggers **N reactive updates**. Each update:
- Recursively walks the object tree for dependency tracking
- Notifies all reactive dependents
- Triggers Vue's reactivity system N times

### After
```typescript
const addUsers = (newUsers: User[]) => {
  // ✅ Batch all updates in memory
  const updates = newUsers.reduce<Record<string, IUser>>((acc, userResponse) => {
    const prevUser = usersById.value[userResponse.id] ?? {};
    const merged = { ...prevUser, ...userResponse };
    acc[userResponse.id] = {
      ...merged,
      fullName: userResponse.firstName
        ? `${merged.firstName} ${merged.lastName || ''}`
        : undefined,
      isDefaultUser: _isDefaultUser(merged),
      isPendingUser: _isPendingUser(merged),
    };
    return acc;
  }, {});

  // ✅ Triggers only ONE reactive update
  usersById.value = { ...usersById.value, ...updates };
};
```

**Performance gain:** Adding N users now triggers **only 1 reactive update**, regardless of N.

## Performance Impact

### Theoretical Analysis
| Metric | Before | After | Improvement |
|--------|--------|-------|-------------|
| Reactive updates for N users | O(N) | O(1) | **N times fewer** |
| Dependency tracking overhead | N times | 1 time | **N times reduction** |
| Update dispatching | N times | 1 time | **N times reduction** |

### Practical Impact
- **Small batches (5-10 users):** Noticeable reduction in reactivity overhead
- **Medium batches (20-50 users):** Significant performance improvement
- **Large batches (100+ users):** Major performance win, prevents UI freezing

This optimization is particularly beneficial when:
- Loading initial user lists in settings
- Bulk inviting users
- Refreshing user data after permissions changes

